  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Total obligations for Service-Disabled Veteran-Owned Small Business set-asides are $6.4B for FY2026 to date, with 12,852 awards and an average award size of $502K. Obligations are down 25.8% year over year. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the dominant buyer, obligating $5.2B. Agency buying outside the VA is led by the Department of Defense at $649M, Department of Homeland Security at $124M, and Department of Transportation at $104M. Construction and IT-related work dominate industry demand: Commercial and Institutional Building Construction accounts for $1.9B, Other Computer Related Services accounts for $1.3B, Custom Computer Programming Services accounts for $448M, and Engineering Services accounts for $344M. The largest vendors in FY2026 to date are V3GATE, LLC at $352M, FOUR POINTS TECHNOLOGY, L.L.C. at $254M, and SPREZZATURA MANAGEMENT CONSULTING, LLC at $214M. The FY2026 to date total of $6.4B sits below recent fiscal-year totals such as $8.7B in FY25 and $8.8B in FY24, reflecting a smaller current-year obligation base compared with prior full fiscal years.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are SDVOSB set-aside obligations across agencies?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs leads agency buying with $5.2B in obligations. The Department of Defense is the second-largest buyer with $649M, followed by Department of Homeland Security at $124M and Department of Transportation at $104M. The top ten agencies show a steep concentration of obligations in a few buyers."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries capture the most SDVOSB set-aside dollars?",
        "body": "Commercial and Institutional Building Construction captures the largest share at $1.9B. Other Computer Related Services follows with $1.3B, while Custom Computer Programming Services and Engineering Services account for $448M and $344M respectively. The industry mix indicates strong demand in construction and IT-related services."
      }
    ]
  },
